## What this project is

{{if .Container -}}
An app forge did not write — a server in any language, with its own Dockerfile — adopted as a forge project without changing a file of it. forge builds the image from that Dockerfile and deploys it: environments, build-once releases promoted by digest, plans, rollout waits, managed Postgres and secrets on Reliant hosting.
{{- else -}}
An existing app, adopted onto forge as it is. forge is here for the pieces this project opted into
{{- if $deploy}} — its environments and deploys{{if .Monitoring}}, and monitoring{{end}}
{{- else if .Monitoring}} — monitoring
{{- else if .Database}} — its database migrations
{{- else}} — so far only `forge.yaml`, its name and switches{{end}} — not to rebuild it.
{{- end}}

**It keeps its own language, layout and tooling.** forge owns only `forge.yaml`
{{- if $deploy}} and `deploy/kcl/`{{end}} (declarations you edit, not generated output), plus whatever the user opts into later. So:

- **Never move existing code into forge's layout** — `internal/handlers/`, `proto/`, `cmd/`, `db/migrations/` — and never rename, restructure or "forge-ify" it, unless the user asks.
- forge's Go-service rules — handlers, proto as the API, the generated ORM, `{{.CLI}} generate` — describe projects forge built, not this one. Do not add a {{if not .GoModule}}`go.mod`, a {{end}}`proto/` or a `Taskfile.yml` to "make forge work": it already does.
- Keep the app's existing deploy YAML, Helm charts, compose files and CI as they are. Translating them into KCL is the user's decision, never a cleanup; forge can deploy hand-written manifests and charts where they stand (`deploy/byo-yaml`).
- **The next pieces are a menu, not a migration.** `{{.CLI}} skill load adopt` lists what forge can take on next — deploys, environments, releases, secrets, migrations, monitoring — each on its own, in any order, with what it takes. Offer one when it would help; start it only when the user asks.

```
<the app>, as it was               ← yours, in its own language; forge never rewrites it
forge.yaml                         ← name, forge pin, harness{{if or .Monitoring .DeclaredEnvs}}, and the switches it turns on{{end}}
{{- if $deploy}}
deploy/kcl/kcl.mod
{{- if or .Workloads .Container}}
deploy/kcl/workloads.k             ← the app, declared ONCE: forge.DockerBuild on the Dockerfile, its port, its probe
{{- end}}
deploy/kcl/<env>/main.k            ← where it runs, one per env: {{if .DeployEnvs}}{{range $i, $e := .DeployEnvs}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}{{$e}}{{end}}{{else}}dev, staging, prod{{end}}
{{- end}}
```

## Ask forge before guessing

- `{{.CLI}} skill list` is the catalog, `{{.CLI}} skill load <name>` prints one (`adopt` first), and `{{.CLI}} skill search <word>` finds one. Skills come from the binary and are never written to disk.
- `{{.CLI}} project capabilities` lists every verb forge has: check it before concluding forge cannot do something.
- Debugging: `{{.CLI}} skill load debug` names the evidence forge keeps{{if $deploy}}; every process `{{.CLI}} env up` runs is teed to `.forge/logs/<env>/<name>.log`, so grep there before guessing{{end}}.
{{- if $deploy}}

## Environments and deploys

The envs are the declaration: `deploy/kcl/<env>/main.k`. Change a binding or an env value there, then check it with `{{.CLI}} env render <env>`. `deploy`, `deploy/hosting` and `secrets` are the skills.

```bash
{{.CLI}} env up dev                     # run dev as declared{{if or .Workloads .Container}}: the app's dev command as a host process, with PORT (and DATABASE_URL) set{{end}}
{{.CLI}} env render <env>               # check an env's declaration
{{.CLI}} env deploy <env>               # build ONCE, push, cut a release, promote it
{{.CLI}} obs check <env> --wait --json  # prove the release good; branch on its exit code
{{.CLI}} env deploy <next-env> <release> # promote the same bytes, by digest — no rebuild
```
{{if or .Workloads .Container}}
The dev command is the `command` in `deploy/kcl/dev/main.k`: replace it with your watch / hot-reload loop.
{{end}}
A hosted deploy needs billing on the Reliant organization; until it is set up `env deploy` exits 7 and prints the billing link. Give the user that link; don't retry.

- The app reads its config from environment variables. A per-env value is `env` on the app in that env's `main.k`; a credential is `env = {KEY = forge.ManagedSecret {name = "KEY"}}` there, with its value set once by `{{.CLI}} secret set --env <env> KEY` (stdin). forge never sees a secret's value.
- A managed database (if declared) reaches the app as `DATABASE_URL` through `forge.DatabaseRef`.
- `{{.CLI}} obs logs <env>` and `{{.CLI}} obs workloads <env>` (restarts, crash loops, failed jobs) work for any app. Request rates, error rates and traces exist only if the app exports OpenTelemetry (OTLP to `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT`, which hosting sets on every workload): until it does, `{{.CLI}} obs check` is INCONCLUSIVE with `reason_code: no_request_telemetry` — not proven good — while restarts, alerts and issues still count. Load `observability/query` for the details.
{{- else if .DeclaredEnvs}}

