## Tests and the gate

{{template "gate-commands" .}}
{{- if and .Gate.Source (ne .Gate.Runner "task")}} There is no Taskfile here, so no `task test`{{if not .GoModule}}, and no Go module, so no `go build`{{end}}.{{end}} There is no `{{.CLI}} test` either.
{{- if $deploy}} Before shipping, `{{.CLI}} lint` checks forge's own files and `{{.CLI}} env render <env>` proves each env still renders.{{end}}

A run expected to take more than ~2 minutes goes in the background (`run_in_background` + `shell_wait`), never a long foreground call — and never detached by hand with `nohup`, a trailing `&`, `setsid` or `disown`.

## Critical rules

- **The app's files are yours.** forge generates no code into this project and never rewrites its source{{if .Container}}, its Dockerfile{{end}} or its build config. What forge owns is `forge.yaml`{{if $deploy}} and `deploy/kcl/`{{end}}.
- **Never move the app into forge's layout**, and never translate its existing config into forge's, unless the user asks.
{{- if .Container}}
- **The Dockerfile must build on its own.** A deploy runs `docker build` with that file and context and nothing else: an image that `COPY`s a `dist/` built on your machine fails there. Prove it with `docker build .` before the first deploy.
{{- end}}
{{- if $deploy}}
- **Gate with `{{.CLI}} lint` once, before you finish**, not after every edit. Never pipe it through `head` — the verdict is the LAST line.
- **Ship releases, not builds.** Build once, then deploy that release to each env in turn.
{{- end}}

